Retriever model Research
    - want to test the best open source retriever and embedding models 
    - problem statement/ situation
        you have a certain text/note and a group of labels/ file names (these files/labels can increase or decrease as time passes by..) 
        you need to place the text/note in one of these files which is the most related to that file/label 
    - Assumptions
        - you do hierarchal filtering - At selecting a file or folder for a given note/text you only deal with max 20 labels at a time..
            - reason for this is this is the max number of files or folder within a folder is just atmost 20 and not more than that.. 
        - assume that the concepts in the file names/labels are mutually exclusive of the concepts which are covered in some other file names
            - you make the system follow this particular design principle to keep the organising of the knowledge base simple and upto the standards
    - challenges in this problem 
        - can differentiate between 2 different labels but the problem is with differentiating 2 similar kind of labels which are soo close to one another in meaning
            - how should you design this problem to address this problem 
            - the problem should 
        - implementation challenges 
            - in a folder you can have different sub-folder names or file names
            - sub-folder names --> can be different from one another if that folder is at the top of the tree knowledge base
            - file names  ---> these can be similar to one another(as they all are in the same folder but at the bottom of the tree knowledge base)
            - the text/ note can be closer to more than one file/folder name /label..... what to do then?? 
            - should you implement a scoring function
            - should you have a threshold to say the confidence 
            - should you follow a selecting function like a softmax to only choose one?? 
    - solutions/ approaches
        - given text, labels ; output need to find the correct match label which is the closest to the text
        - semantic search over the current labels (embedding + similarity function)
            convert the text to its embedding vector  (will change everytime so you have to compute this everytime)
            convert the labels to embeddings    (one time thing as the labels won't change drastically everytime)
            choose a similarity function like cosine similarity
            compare the similarity of each of text, label pair => similarity(text, label) and choose the one with the highest similarity score
        - can we compare with different similarity functions??
            difference between dot product and cosine similarity 
        - NLI (natural language inference)
            - input 
                - text and label sentence mixed as hypothesis sent to the model
                - text/notes ---> premise 
                - this text/notes is realted to the label "xyz" ---> hypothesis
            - output 
                - it gives probabilities for these three: entailment, neutral, contradiction
            - process   
                - model takes the (input hypothesis); passes them through transformers and then will have a softmax classification head at the end to give the probabilities of the 3 classes
            - you select the lable with the highest entailment probability
            - can be slow to get the results as we have to pass the labels everytime with a new text/notes to get the inference 
            - but has greater accuracy than semantic search as we have attention to look up the relation between both.. 
        - cross encoder similarity 
            - it is similar to the above approach but instead of getting probability scores you will get a similarity score 
            - model(text + label) --> similarity score 
            - input: [CLS] text [Sep] label; label description [Sep]
            - you pass the input through transformer blocks and at the end you will have a pooling function; then finally you have a regression head which gives a similarity score as output
            - pooling function 
                - cls token:
                - vector addition of all the tokens at the end of the transformers blocks: 
            - this helps in capturing deep interaction through attention rather than with embedding in the case of semantic search
            - bi-encoder → compare vectors
            - cross-encoder → jointly understand text + label
        - contrastive learning models 
            - to pull similar labels together and push different ones apart
            - this also helps to better separate similar closely associated labels ?????
            - problem you have to put the text in a folder at the leaf of the knowledge base and you have similar file names in that folder ----> how to increase the contrast to select the best file label for that text???
                - train 2 separate models??
            - SimCSE, CLIP style architectures... 
            - is contrastive learning models only different based on the type of the data used and the training methods and the loss functions or is there any difference in the architectural pattern too???
        -  clustering and then labeling notes/text 
            - group similar texts and then assign labels to the formed clusters... 
            - this is more of an unsupervised approach.. but we are looking into to get a supervised labeled data which is to follow user specific file arranging system depending on his preference 
            - for this clustering to work you already need to have a well established dataset to train the model on the clustering to get a good idea of how text should be encoded to go near a specific cluster
            - classify new text by nearest cluster 
            - good for discovering new labels 
        - sparse retireval models 
            - instead of dense embeddings used in search systems... you can simply have a simple binary representation of the embeddings 
            - this is exteremely useful if you have large label space
    - prerequisites
        - how embedding models work and what does its architecture look like and what kind of loss function is used here? and how is training done and how should the dataset look like if we want to create one using this application 
        - any other alternatives to embedding models in this use case of this application? 
    - intelligent smart things to implement:
        - instead of just implementing a single solution/ approach you can mix up 2 or more of these implementations to perform better 
        - each label should have a textual description to provide better context when comparing labels with the text or when converting the label to an embedding... for folder names this can also include all the file names present inside it and some other context about what kind of data is present in that file/folder
